# Documentation

## Principles

- Documentation MUST be **concise**: short sentences, no filler, no duplicated code comments.
- Documentation MUST stay **current**: update docs in the same change that alters behavior, APIs, or setup.
- Prefer **official framework documentation** when citing external behavior (Spring, React, PostgreSQL, etc.).
- Do not document what the code already states clearly; document **why** and **non-obvious constraints**.

## What to maintain

| Artifact | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `README.md` | Quick start, prerequisites, run/test commands, env vars (names only, no secrets) |
| OpenAPI / springdoc | Live API contract; must match controllers |
| `.specify/` specs (`spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md`) | Feature intent before implementation |
| `constitution.md` | Project governance; amend when principles change |
| Liquibase changelogs | Schema history; include brief change comment per migration |

## Style

- Use imperative headings: "Run tests", not "How to run tests".
- One topic per section; link to official docs for deep reference instead of copying them.
- Examples MUST be copy-pasteable and verified against the current codebase.
- Use tables and lists only when they reduce length; avoid narrative duplication of code.
- No committed secrets, tokens, or real credentials in any doc.

## When code changes

Update documentation if the change affects any of:

- Public API paths, request/response shapes, or status codes
- Environment variables or local setup steps
- Business rules, state machine, or RAG behavior described for users/operators
- Dependencies, versions, or required tools

If behavior changes but docs do not, treat the PR as incomplete.

## Comments in code

- Java: Javadoc only on public APIs and complex domain rules; skip getters/setters and obvious DTOs.
- TypeScript: TSDoc only where types alone are insufficient.
- Never leave `TODO` in docs without an owner or linked task; remove obsolete TODOs when done.

## Out of scope

- Do not add markdown files the user did not ask for (no drive-by READMEs or design docs).
- Do not paste full tutorials; link to authoritative sources.
